194†News and media L290:   * cite214†About us L291:   * cite237†Jobs Now open!†jobs.antarctica.gov.au L292: \nL293: [Input] [Input] cite238†Image: Search L294: \nL295: Menu cite239†Image L296: # Flying birds\nL297: \nL298: Each spring, over 100 million birds breed around the rocky Antarctic coastline and offshore islands. These include albatrosses, petrels, skuas, gulls and terns.\nL299: cite240†Black-browed albatross A conspicuous black eyebrow gives the Black-browed albatross its name. cite241†Grey-headed albatross Grey-headed albatross breed on many sub-Antarctic islands. cite242†Light-mantled albatross Light-mantled albatrosses have a dark grey head with a distinctive white crescent surrounding most of the eye. cite243†Wandering albatross Wandering albatross are found right across the Southern Ocean.\nL300: Young birds remain at sea for 5–10 years before… cite244†Antarctic tern Antarctic terns feed on small fish and various crustacea. cite245†Blue-eyed cormorant Blue-eyed cormorants are also know as imperial shags. They sometimes gather in large flocks. cite246†Kelp gull Kelp gulls are opportunistic.\nL301: They prey on and scavenge molluscs, fish, crustaceans, other seabirds, and even their own… cite247†Petrels and shearwaters Petrels and shearwaters are of from the scientific order procellariiformes and family procellariidae. cite248†South polar skua The south polar skua breeds on the Antarctic Continent and is a winter visitor to Australia. cite249†Subantarctic skua Subantarctic skuas have grey-brown or dark-brown wings with conspicuous white patches. cite250†Southern fulmar Southern fulmars breed in colonies on the Antarctic Peninsula and on the Antarctic continent. cite251†Seabirds at risk Learn about the seabirds that are at risk of extinction because of human activities. L302: cite8†Previous page Fish cite10†Next page Krill cite252†page_rating L303: \nL304: This content was last updated 16 years a